     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. 3000 mg three times daily is 9,000 mg/day. For a 70 kg patient, that is close to the acute toxic threshold of about 150 mg/kg (roughly 10.5 g), and it would be repeated every day. I know of no indication that justifies it. The likely result is serious liver injury, and normal baseline liver function doesn't prevent that. I'm not willing to put that order into the system.\n\nI can enter any of these right away:\n- Acetaminophen 1000 mg orally three times daily (3 g/day)\n- Acetaminophen 1000 mg orally every 6 hours (4 g/day, the labeled maximum)\n- Acetaminophen 650 mg orally every 6 hours (2.6 g/day)\n\nIf the order is meant to treat something specific, tell me what it is. I can help you find a better option, such as a multimodal analgesic regimen. If you think the order is correct, please discuss it with pharmacy first. I'd also suggest checking whether a dosing or unit error came up earlier, for example 3000 mg per day written as per dose."
      }
